<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wushe Incident</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1930">1930</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Musha Incident</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wushe Incident (Musha Incident) was an uprising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seediq-people">Seediq people</a> against Japanese colonial rule at Wushe (Musha) in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> in October 1930. Led by the chief Mona Rudao, warriors attacked a school sports meeting and killed more than 130 Japanese. The authorities crushed the revolt with troops, aircraft and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poison-gas">poison gas</a>, and hundreds of Seediq were killed or took their own lives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wushe-incident.jpg" alt="Wushe Incident" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_scene_of_the_Wushe_Incident.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Earlier armed resistance to Japanese rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, such as the Tapani Incident, had been met with harsh retaliation, and by the 1930s the younger Taiwanese generation had largely turned to organised political and social movements. The indigenous peoples, however, were still classed as seiban, raw barbarians, and treated as inferiors. They were disarmed and forced to settle as farmers, and Governor-General Sakuma Samata's five-year plan had sought to pacify or eradicate the groups living in areas marked for logging.</p><p>The Seediq of Musha were held up as a model of this policy, and Mona Rudao had been one of 43 indigenous leaders taken on a tour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Resentment remained over police misconduct, forced labour and disrespect for Seediq customs. Days before the attack, at the wedding of Mona Rudao's son Daho Mona, the police officer Katsuhiko Yoshimura refused a cup of wine and struck Daho Mona with his stick; a fight followed, and Mona Rudao's apology was turned away.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 27 October 1930 hundreds of Japanese gathered at the elementary school for an athletics meet. Shortly before dawn Mona Rudao led over 300 warriors against police sub-stations to seize weapons and ammunition, then attacked the school, while other groups raided thirteen police stations, post offices, dormitories and shops and cut the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> lines and roads. The attackers killed 134 Japanese, among them women and children, and two Han Taiwanese mistaken for Japanese; most of the victims were beheaded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Governor-General Ishizuka Eizō imposed a press blackout and sent two thousand troops. The Seediq withdrew into the mountains and fought by night, and the army air corps dropped mustard gas bombs, allegedly the first such use of chemical warfare in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Mona Rudao killed himself on 28 November, and remaining resistance was suppressed by the third week of December. Of the 1,200 Seediq directly involved, 644 died, 290 of them by suicide.</p><p>Criticism forced Ishizuka and his chief civil administrator Hitomi Jirō to resign in January 1931. On 25 April 1931 indigenous groups working with the Japanese attacked the village where about 500 surrendered Seediq were confined and beheaded every male over 15, the Second Musha Incident. Policy then shifted: the indigenous peoples were recast as imperial subjects under the name takasagozoku, and part of the Seediq land went to their rivals the Truku and Toda. The uprising is the subject of the 2011 film Seediq Bale.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Musha Incident” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Resistance continued, notably the Tapani incident of 1915 and the Seediq <b>uprising at Musha</b> in 1930.</blockquote><a href="/state/taiwan-under-japanese-rule">Taiwan under Japanese rule</a></li></ul></section>
